Your working tree has 4 more files mid-fix. I read every diff — they're done, not half-finished:

**1. N+1 sequential AI calls** — `src/lib/ai/eval-runner.server.ts:157-226`
Was: `for (const c of cases) { await callModel(...subject); await callModel(...judge); }` — fully serial.
Now: bounded-concurrency fan-out.
```ts
const limit = pLimit(4);
const caseResults = await Promise.all(
  cases.map((c) => limit(async () => { /* subject + judge calls */ })),
);
```
~4x wall-clock speedup on eval suites, plus the earlier batch-insert is preserved.

**2. N+1 writes** — `src/lib/ai/assumption-watch.server.ts:131-190`
Was: one `update` + one conditional `insert` per assumption, in a loop.
Now: judgments run under `pLimit(4)`, then all writes batch:
```ts
if (watchedIds.length > 0) {
  await supabase.from("assumptions").update({ last_watched_at: nowIso }).in("id", watchedIds);
}
if (challengeInserts.length > 0) {
  await supabase.from("assumption_challenges").insert(challengeInserts);
}
```

**3. N+1 writes** — `src/lib/ai/supersession.server.ts:109-150`
Per-edge `upsert` in a loop → one batched `upsert(edges, {...})`, with the reverse-pair invalidation updates parallelized via `Promise.all`.

**4. N+1 writes** — `src/lib/meetings.functions.ts:189-210`
Was: `for (const d of insertedDecisions) { await recordStageEvent(...) }` — one insert per decision.
Now: rows accumulated and inserted in one call to `stage_events`.

These four are ready — worth running `bun run build` + the relevant test files and committing when you're ready (didn't commit since you didn't ask).

**N+1 insert** — `src/lib/ai/tools/registry.server.ts:1610-1625` (the `studio.commit` / Build file-claim tool):
```ts
for (const path of paths) {
  if (ours.has(path)) continue;
  const { error } = await supabase.from("builder_file_claims").insert({ ...one row per path... });
  if (error && !/unique|duplicate/i.test(error.message)) { ... }
}
```
Every Build commit does one sequential insert per changed file — a 15-file commit is 15 round trips in the hot path of an agent's edit loop. Fix (mirrors the pattern already used 4x above tonight):
```ts
const toClaim = paths.filter((p) => !ours.has(p));
if (toClaim.length > 0) {
  const { error } = await supabase.from("builder_file_claims").insert(
    toClaim.map((path) => ({
      user_id: userId, workspace_id: workspaceId ?? null, run_id: runId ?? null,
      mission_id: missionId, mission_title: missionTitle, repo, path, status: "held",
    })),
  );
  if (error && !/unique|duplicate/i.test(error.message)) {
    console.error("[studio.commit] claim insert failed:", error.message);
  }
}
```
Note: unique-constraint conflicts on individual rows currently fall through silently per-row; a batch insert fails the whole batch on any single conflict. Given this only runs after the `held`-by-others check above, a same-mission race is the only realistic conflict source — acceptable trade, same reasoning already applied to `assumption_challenges` above.

`resolveProviderAuth` (`src/lib/connectors/resolve.server.ts`) has **zero in-process caching** — confirmed via grep, no `Map`/`TTL`/`cache` anywhere in the file. It's the credential chokepoint for every external connector call (GitHub, Slack, Linear, etc.), re-resolving workspace binding → user connection → env fallback on every invocation. `runtime.server.ts` already has the exact pattern to copy:
```ts
let _creditsEnabledCache: { value: boolean; at: number } | null = null;
const CREDITS_FLAG_TTL_MS = 5 * 60 * 1000;
async function creditsEnabled(supabase: SupabaseClient): Promise<boolean> {
  const now = Date.now();
  if (_creditsEnabledCache && now - _creditsEnabledCache.at < CREDITS_FLAG_TTL_MS) {
    return _creditsEnabledCache.value;
  }
  // ...resolve, then _creditsEnabledCache = { value, at: now };
}
```
Same shape works for provider auth, keyed by `(workspaceId, providerId)`.

